1
00:00:11,850 --> 00:00:13,350
What's up preppies?

2
00:00:13,850 --> 00:00:14,890
Hello everyone.

3
00:00:15,590 --> 00:00:19,410
Welcome back to the next lesson on IELTS Writing Task 2.

4
00:00:19,710 --> 00:00:24,770
Today we are looking at how to turn your ideas into a higher score.

5
00:00:25,190 --> 00:00:28,150
Here is the writing prompt we are working with today.

6
00:00:28,470 --> 00:00:34,330
In the modern world, people of all ages have access to various types of media.

7
00:00:34,330 --> 00:00:38,770
What are the advantages and disadvantages of this trend?

8
00:00:39,170 --> 00:00:41,930
I want you to pause this video for one minute.

9
00:00:42,210 --> 00:00:42,950
Grab a pen.

10
00:00:43,210 --> 00:00:46,330
Think about every idea you have for this topic.

11
00:00:46,790 --> 00:00:47,570
Let's go!

12
00:00:51,940 --> 00:00:56,180
Okay, I bet your paper looks something like this.

13
00:00:56,420 --> 00:00:57,240
You have a list.

14
00:00:57,480 --> 00:01:04,180
Maybe you wrote down educational for kids, fun, or prevents loneliness for the elderly.

15
00:01:04,180 --> 00:01:07,160
You look at this list and think, great!

16
00:01:07,680 --> 00:01:09,160
I have 10 ideas.

17
00:01:09,580 --> 00:01:11,500
I should put them all in my essay.

18
00:01:12,020 --> 00:01:17,840
But this leads us to the two most important questions for your task response score.

19
00:01:18,520 --> 00:01:19,380
Question 1.

20
00:01:20,060 --> 00:01:22,880
Should you put all your ideas into your essay?

21
00:01:23,640 --> 00:01:24,600
Question 2.

22
00:01:25,160 --> 00:01:27,580
In what way should you arrange your ideas?

23
00:01:28,260 --> 00:01:29,440
Let's find out.

24
00:01:33,300 --> 00:01:37,580
Now, let's see how this goes wrong in an actual essay.

25
00:01:37,840 --> 00:01:41,920
Here is an example paragraph about media access for teenagers.

26
00:01:42,700 --> 00:01:43,740
Let's take a look.

27
00:01:50,580 --> 00:01:53,440
At first glance, this looks impressive, right?

28
00:01:53,940 --> 00:01:54,840
It is long.

29
00:01:55,400 --> 00:02:00,820
It uses high-level vocabulary like consumption and vast amount.

30
00:02:01,380 --> 00:02:03,680
And look at those transition words.

31
00:02:03,680 --> 00:02:11,240
Firstly, secondly, in addition, furthermore, moreover, finally.

32
00:02:12,040 --> 00:02:13,280
It feels organized.

33
00:02:14,300 --> 00:02:16,960
But the examiner sees something different.

34
00:02:17,720 --> 00:02:22,120
They see a list of six separate ideas put into one paragraph.

35
00:02:23,160 --> 00:02:24,780
1. Education.

36
00:02:25,600 --> 00:02:27,100
2. Entertainment.

37
00:02:28,120 --> 00:02:30,040
3. Connection.

38
00:02:30,520 --> 00:02:32,380
4. News.

39
00:02:32,820 --> 00:02:34,460
5. Skills.

40
00:02:35,160 --> 00:02:37,160
6. Shopping.

41
00:02:37,780 --> 00:02:40,780
The examiner is left asking, so what?

42
00:02:41,380 --> 00:02:43,500
How does learning coding help them?

43
00:02:44,080 --> 00:02:46,380
Does it improve their future career prospects?

44
00:02:47,140 --> 00:02:49,940
Does it make them more likely to get a better job?

45
00:02:50,500 --> 00:02:54,860
Because you didn't explain anything, this idea is not yet developed.

46
00:02:55,760 --> 00:03:01,260
This paragraph tries to cover a lot of ideas, but its depth is severely lacking.

47
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:06,740
Even with a great vocabulary, this kind of structure usually limits your

48
00:03:06,740 --> 00:03:10,100
task response score to a band 5 or 6.

49
00:03:10,760 --> 00:03:21,700
To fix this, we need to cut 5 of these ideas, pick the best one, and expand it in a better way with the Cause, Benefit, Consequence strategy.

50
00:03:22,400 --> 00:03:24,440
Let's find out what it is with me.

51
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:34,640
So, how do we fix a paragraph that has too many ideas but no depth?

52
00:03:34,940 --> 00:03:39,240
We need to stop listing and start building logical chains of ideas.

53
00:03:39,820 --> 00:03:45,120
To do this, we will use the Cause, Benefit, Consequence strategy.

54
00:03:45,580 --> 00:03:52,180
Let's go back to that long paragraph from the previous section and pick just one idea to save.

55
00:03:52,760 --> 00:03:53,740
Let's choose.

56
00:03:54,240 --> 00:03:56,640
Learning digital skills like coding.

57
00:03:57,270 --> 00:04:00,680
We are going to remove the other 5 ideas entirely.

58
00:04:01,620 --> 00:04:04,600
Now, let's expand this using our strategy.

59
00:04:05,440 --> 00:04:07,320
We start with the Core, Benefit.

60
00:04:07,580 --> 00:04:10,100
Teenagers are able to learn new skills.

61
00:04:10,480 --> 00:04:14,360
But before we write that down, we must ask the How question.

62
00:04:14,820 --> 00:04:17,680
How is this possible today compared to the past?

63
00:04:18,640 --> 00:04:20,680
Is it because schools are teaching more?

64
00:04:20,899 --> 00:04:21,740
Not necessarily.

65
00:04:22,200 --> 00:04:24,680
Is it because they are buying expensive textbooks?

66
00:04:25,500 --> 00:04:25,940
No.

67
00:04:26,720 --> 00:04:28,620
The real key is accessibility.

68
00:04:29,460 --> 00:04:31,540
The barrier to entry has disappeared.

69
00:04:32,120 --> 00:04:38,580
Specifically, modern media platforms host millions of high-quality tutorials for free.

70
00:04:39,200 --> 00:04:41,280
So, our cause is specific.

71
00:04:41,720 --> 00:04:44,820
The availability of free accessible tutorials.

72
00:04:45,260 --> 00:04:48,540
This accessibility is the foundation of your argument.

73
00:04:49,120 --> 00:04:52,020
Without free tutorials, the benefit collapses.

74
00:04:52,640 --> 00:04:55,180
But with them, the benefit is supported.

75
00:04:56,040 --> 00:04:58,800
So, we place our benefit on top of the cause.

76
00:04:59,500 --> 00:05:02,120
So, how do we turn this into an essay?

77
00:05:02,820 --> 00:05:04,520
It is a simple translation.

78
00:05:05,400 --> 00:05:09,440
The top brick, the benefit, becomes your topic sentence.

79
00:05:10,260 --> 00:05:12,720
This is the claim you are making to the examiner.

80
00:05:13,280 --> 00:05:17,940
And the bottom brick, the foundation, becomes your elaboration.

81
00:05:18,880 --> 00:05:22,300
This is the evidence that proves your claim is true.

82
00:05:22,980 --> 00:05:29,420
So, by combining them, we get the first half of a perfect band 6 to 7 paragraph.

83
00:05:30,380 --> 00:05:32,380
First, the topic sentence.

84
00:05:32,980 --> 00:05:37,780
The most significant advantage of digital media for young people is the

85
00:05:37,780 --> 00:05:42,000
ability to acquire new technical skills all by themselves.

86
00:05:42,800 --> 00:05:46,100
Next, the elaboration or the foundation.

87
00:05:46,100 --> 00:05:49,120
Let's keep it clear and simple.

88
00:05:49,860 --> 00:05:56,780
This is mainly because the internet provides instant access to a huge amount of free educational content.

89
00:05:57,620 --> 00:06:02,290
Modern online platforms host step-by-step video guides on difficult subjects

90
00:06:02,600 --> 00:06:05,880
such as video editing or computer programming.

91
00:06:06,760 --> 00:06:08,060
And there you have it.

92
00:06:08,280 --> 00:06:10,660
No listing, no random ideas.

93
00:06:11,040 --> 00:06:14,280
Just a clear claim supported by a solid reason.

94
00:06:15,160 --> 00:06:16,980
Now, what happens next?

95
00:06:17,560 --> 00:06:20,080
What is the consequence or result of this?

96
00:06:20,720 --> 00:06:22,080
Let's finish the chain.

97
00:06:22,960 --> 00:06:25,640
Why does it matter that teenagers learn these skills?

98
00:06:26,200 --> 00:06:27,120
What is the outcome?

99
00:06:27,980 --> 00:06:30,000
Think about the modern job market.

100
00:06:30,460 --> 00:06:32,740
Employers today don't just want degrees.

101
00:06:33,340 --> 00:06:35,540
They are looking for practical skill sets.

102
00:06:35,840 --> 00:06:42,160
They need people who can actually build websites, design graphics or edit video right now.

103
00:06:42,940 --> 00:06:47,920
Since these teenagers already know what companies use and need, they fit

104
00:06:47,920 --> 00:06:50,280
into the modern workforce much better.

105
00:06:51,020 --> 00:06:54,200
They aren't just students, they are skilled candidates.

106
00:06:54,880 --> 00:06:57,500
So, let's write the final sentence simply.

107
00:06:58,420 --> 00:07:04,540
This allows students to master these skills at their own speed without needing to pay for a teacher.

108
00:07:05,220 --> 00:07:09,440
As a result, they are much better prepared for their future careers,

109
00:07:09,440 --> 00:07:12,840
which increases their chances of finding a good job.

110
00:07:13,420 --> 00:07:15,200
Now, look at the full picture.

111
00:07:15,940 --> 00:07:17,760
We didn't list five different things.

112
00:07:18,340 --> 00:07:22,340
We took one idea, learning skills, and we followed it from the

113
00:07:22,340 --> 00:07:28,160
cause or elaboration to the advantage and finally all the way to the result.

114
00:07:28,660 --> 00:07:31,980
This is what a band six to seven paragraph looks like.

115
00:07:36,070 --> 00:07:37,070
Great job today.

116
00:07:37,570 --> 00:07:40,070
Let's recap the golden rule of this lesson.

117
00:07:40,070 --> 00:07:47,390
At the start, we saw that having a list of five or six random ideas does not mean you have a good essay.

118
00:07:47,870 --> 00:07:49,290
That is just listing.

119
00:07:49,890 --> 00:07:58,290
To get a higher score, you need to stop listing and start building proper paragraphs with well-connected and well-explained ideas.

120
00:07:58,870 --> 00:08:03,350
You learned that one strong idea built on a solid foundation is

121
00:08:03,350 --> 00:08:05,470
worth more than five weak ones.

122
00:08:06,010 --> 00:08:10,610
So, when you put your pen to paper, follow this exact sequence.

123
00:08:11,450 --> 00:08:16,010
One, the topic sentence, the advantage or the disadvantage.

124
00:08:16,710 --> 00:08:18,410
Start with your main claim.

125
00:08:19,290 --> 00:08:22,070
Two, the elaboration, the cause.

126
00:08:22,650 --> 00:08:26,990
Immediately support it with your because or due to foundation.

127
00:08:27,710 --> 00:08:30,130
Three, the result, the consequence.

128
00:08:31,030 --> 00:08:32,929
Finish with the outcome that matters.

129
00:08:33,370 --> 00:08:36,470
And now, I'll see you in the next video.

130
00:08:36,470 --> 00:08:37,610
See ya!

